<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahakam River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahakam River is the main river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, flowing about 900 kilometres from the interior highlands to the Makassar Strait. Its valley was the home of the early kingdom of Kutai, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> yupa inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muara-kaman">Muara Kaman</a> are among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and later of the Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate. Samarinda stands near its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakam-river.jpg" alt="Mahakam River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herusutimbul · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kapal_Tongkang_Batubara_melintas_Sungai_Mahakam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mahakam is the third longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapuas-river">Kapuas</a> and the Barito, and the largest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a>, with a catchment of about 77,100 square kilometres. It rises at Cemaru in the highlands of the island, flows south-east to Muara Pahu and then east through a low, flat lake region with about 30 shallow lakes, of which Jempang, Semayang and Melintang are the largest.</p><p>Below the lakes it is joined by the Belayan, Kedang Kepala and Kedang Rantau and enters the Makassar Strait through a delta of about 1,800 square kilometres, with mangroves near the shore, Nypa swamps in the middle and lowland forest near the apex. Samarinda lies about 48 kilometres from the mouth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Mahakam has served as a waterway and as a source of fish and fresh water since ancient times. The kingdom of Kutai grew up in its basin, and its history is divided into two periods. Kutai Martadipura, about 350 to 400 CE, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulavarman">Mulawarman</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muara-kaman">Muara Kaman</a> and is regarded as the oldest kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><p>Kutai Kartanegara, about 1300, was founded by settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> at Kutai Lama near the river's mouth. About 1565 two preachers from Java, Tunggang Parangan and Ri Bandang, spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> widely in Kartanegara. The Kutais, the Banjars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayaks</a> are the peoples of the region.</p><p>Geological investigations began in the lower Mahakam in 1888, and in 1897 exploration drilling found oil at a depth of 46 metres on the Louise structure; production began in 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Mahakam and its floodplain are an ecologically important region, with 147 freshwater fish species and the Irrawaddy dolphin, called pesut locally, and 298 bird species have been recorded in the basin. Fishing is the main livelihood in the lakes area. From the 1970s the Indonesian government moved people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a>, especially near the river, and by 1973 almost 26 per cent of the cultivated land of the province was worked by migrants. The Kutai Kartanegara Bridge over the river collapsed on 26 November 2011, and a new bridge opened on 8 December 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahakam River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seven stone yupa, sacrificial posts inscribed in Sanskrit with Pallava script, were found at Muara Kaman on the <b>Mahakam River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kutai">Kingdom of Kutai</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Salasilah Kutai chronicle, the kingdom was founded around 1300 at Kutai Lama near the mouth of the <b>Mahakam River</b> by Aji Batara Agung Dewa Sakti.</blockquote><a href="/state/kutai-kartanegara-sultanate">Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
